Interior illustration of a dark cellar with a prisoner tied to a chair. Dramatic scene, viewed from slightly above (as if descending stairs and seeing the scene for the first time).

SCENE: A stone cellar, dimly lit by a single hanging lantern. Center of the frame: a thin, middle-aged man tied to a wooden chair with rough rope. He has a black eye, a split lip, and is trembling. His clothes (artisan's apron, simple shirt) are torn. Paint stains on his fingers — he's an artist.

AROUND HIM: Three men in leather jerkins and a woman with a short sword stand in a loose semicircle. The largest man (thick mustache, sergeant bearing) leans toward the prisoner, one hand on the chair back. The woman stands apart, arms crossed, watching coldly. The other two guards flank the scene.

ENVIRONMENT: Wine barrels along the walls, cobwebs, damp stone. The lantern casts harsh shadows — faces half-lit, half in darkness. A small table nearby with rope, a knife, and a bucket of water.

LIGHTING: Single warm lantern from above-center. Strong contrast — bright on the prisoner's face, deep shadows everywhere else. Chiaroscuro.

STYLE: Dramatic fantasy illustration, painterly, dark palette (deep browns, greys, amber lantern light). Think Caravaggio lighting in a fantasy setting. Tense, threatening atmosphere.

MOOD: The moment before rescue. Tension. The prisoner has given up hope. The interrogators are losing patience.
